WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.060
All right, hello everyone.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.050
We have two second year students here.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:09.490
Tal Lee Anderman and Omid Sheybani,
here with us today.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.564
Hello Tal Lee and Omid, so
excited to have both of you here.

00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:14.190
&gt;&gt; Thanks.
&gt;&gt; Thank you for joining us.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.240
So can we start by you both
briefly introducing yourselves?

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.110
And talk a little bit
about your background.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.430
So what were you each doing
before business school?

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.940
And what are your goals after graduation?

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.760
And then we can open up to some
questions from the audience.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.650
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:29.682
&gt;&gt; Tal Lee?
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:00:29.682 --> 00:00:30.870
&gt;&gt; So before the GSB,

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:35.410
I was working in the nonprofit space
at the Environmental Defense Fund.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.690
I focused on sustainable agriculture,
mostly in India.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:41.700
And then later on domestic
environmental issues.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.068
I came to the GSB to work on
corporate sustainability and

00:00:45.068 --> 00:00:47.944
I'm hoping to go into
that post-graduation.

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:48.513
&gt;&gt; And Omid?

00:00:48.513 --> 00:00:52.740
&gt;&gt; Yes, I'm originally from Germany.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.716
I graduated from undergrad for business
degree in Germany and Argentina and

00:00:57.716 --> 00:01:00.560
started working for Google in Ireland.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.320
Was there for two years.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.720
Then I transferred to the US,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.580
was another four years in the US working
on the Latin American market doing

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.810
cloud computing partnerships, distribution
partnerships across Latin America.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.751
Which was a great time, but
I felt like I was ready for some, for

00:01:16.751 --> 00:01:19.370
the next kind of phase of growth.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.910
And that's when I decided to apply
to business school and came here.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:27.637
And very much with the goal of pursuing
entrepreneurial endeavours and

00:01:27.637 --> 00:01:32.206
that's kind of what I'm doing as
we're approaching the last seven,

00:01:32.206 --> 00:01:35.218
eight months of our time
as second year MBAs.

00:01:35.218 --> 00:01:36.330
&gt;&gt; Awesome, great.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.550
Okay, let's look at a couple of questions.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:46.150
So first, our first question is actually
coming from a college senior from India.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.270
This person writes, I am applying for
the Stanford MBA deferred enrollment.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.100
What are the variety of qualities that are
looked into an applicant applying directly

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.160
from college for
the deferred enrollment program?

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.350
So I can take this one.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.960
So we do encourage college
seniors to apply to our program.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.110
That is the earliest time that you
are able to apply to the MBA program.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.850
And we are looking for
the exact same things.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.720
We are looking for the intellectual
vitality, the demonstrated

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.160
leadership essential, as well as your
personal qualities and contributions.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.000
We take all of that into consideration and

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.020
consider the fact that
you are a college senior.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.440
So we are still looking for
the leadership.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.490
But maybe instead of a full time job,
maybe we are able to see that in your

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:29.030
extra curricular activities that
you've been able to do on campus.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.976
Or any work in your community or
if you've been able to do research or

00:02:32.976 --> 00:02:35.139
any internships that you've have.

00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:38.704
So we are still looking for those same
qualities in all of our people who

00:02:38.704 --> 00:02:41.250
are applying for
deferred enrollment, as well.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.221
Our next question for

00:02:44.221 --> 00:02:48.833
our students is what did each of you
do to fill your global requirement?

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:49.950
You want to start, Omid?

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:50.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, happy to.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:55.590
So before school even began,
we had a new program.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.980
It was a pilot when I started.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.190
It was called the Global Seminar and it
was kind of two weeks before school began

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.730
and with I think,
about 25 other incoming MBA1 students.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.507
We went to South Korea,
it was a trip led by two MBA2s, so

00:03:10.507 --> 00:03:13.310
students that were a year ahead of us.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:18.050
And that in itself was sufficient
to satisfy the goal requirement.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:23.201
But I really personally believe that
the trips you do with your fellow MBA

00:03:23.201 --> 00:03:29.115
classmates are not only some of the most
memorable experiences from our time here,

00:03:29.115 --> 00:03:32.510
but also incredible
learning opportunities.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.679
I feel like,
given the depth of these trips, and

00:03:35.679 --> 00:03:40.553
the intensity at which you're getting
to know a country and its economy and

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:44.050
its business landscape,
I really wanted to do more.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.072
So not only did I go on
another trip to China,

00:03:47.072 --> 00:03:52.409
I also ended up currently organizing
a study trip to Italy coming up in March.

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:53.676
So that was my experience.

00:03:53.676 --> 00:03:54.211
&gt;&gt; Great, awesome.

00:03:54.211 --> 00:03:58.753
&gt;&gt; Similarly, I was on a global seminar
before the school year started, so

00:03:58.753 --> 00:04:00.990
that was for me to Brazil.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:04.650
And it was an incredible opportunity to
connect really deeply with my classmates,

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:05.760
before even coming to school.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.100
And we had this wonderful
kind of safety net and

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.248
community as we were
adjusting to life at the GSB.

00:04:11.248 --> 00:04:15.340
It was led by two second year students and
they were a wonderful resource as all of

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.630
the questions start pouring in,
when you get here.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.030
It was also a wonderful
opportunity to learn about Brazil.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.370
I had never been, and so
our subject was poverty alleviation and

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.930
environmental sustainability,
which was very relevant to me.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:30.270
So we met with people across
the nonprofits, in politics, and

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.860
then in corporations.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.600
So we got to learn a lot.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.740
I likewise couldn't get enough,
although that did fulfill the requirement,

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:41.370
and so I went on a global study
trip to Mexico over Christmas.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.540
And something that the global
study trips really highlight is

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.220
the value of the network within the GSB.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.231
So our second year trip leaders were able
to organize meetings with the President of

00:04:50.231 --> 00:04:53.554
Mexico, with the Foreign Minister,
with the head of Central Bank,

00:04:53.554 --> 00:04:56.962
then a variety of really interesting
nonprofits and entrepreneurs.

00:04:56.962 --> 00:04:58.066
Still couldn't get enough [COUGH].

00:04:58.066 --> 00:04:58.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Excuse me.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.890
So I actually led, the Global Seminar
this summer, to Argentina and Peru, and

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:06.260
was able to create that experience for
incoming MBA1s.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:10.410
&gt;&gt; And this experience of doing more than
one global experience is quite common for

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.100
our students, so great.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.980
Okay, the next question we have is,
what is life like for

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.926
students bringing their partners or
families with them?

00:05:17.926 --> 00:05:21.516
If you have personal experience or

00:05:21.516 --> 00:05:25.980
even from your classmates' experience.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.946
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So the truth is,

00:05:27.946 --> 00:05:32.160
we both came here as not married, singles.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.338
We do however, have a lot of classmates
that either came with their SOs which

00:05:36.338 --> 00:05:38.560
stands for Significant Others.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.350
Or without their SO but with the children,
we have those cases as well.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:49.330
I would generally like to highlight
that the GSB community given it's

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:53.600
relatively small size in comparison
to other schools is a very inclusive

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.610
community where you'll very often
find other students reaching out and

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:02.370
trying to help and
by trying to help a bit.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:06.710
I heard just yesterday I was sitting over
dinner, the Germans were coming together

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.690
last night to celebrate
our cultural heritage.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.700
And one of my classmates was talking.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:14.610
She has three children.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:18.400
And she was talking about how
her husband had never actually

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.070
sat in one of the GSB classes.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:22.120
And I asked why.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.290
And she said, well,
because of babysitting, and

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:28.880
I said I'd love to be your babysitter so
that your husband can attend the class.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.630
I think it's just a very inclusive
community and, truth be spoken,

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.610
I think the experience
is slightly different.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.569
You just have a different
priority if you have a husband or

00:06:39.569 --> 00:06:41.641
a wife at home, or children.

00:06:41.641 --> 00:06:46.122
But I think still there are many
ways you can get a lot of

00:06:46.122 --> 00:06:49.121
learnings out of this experience.

00:06:49.121 --> 00:06:52.750
And yeah, it's just a slightly
different one, admittedly.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.320
&gt;&gt; Well, I had a couple of small pieces.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.344
I think a lot of the families
who do come here,

00:06:56.344 --> 00:06:59.275
they live in graduate student housing and
they do nanny shares and

00:06:59.275 --> 00:07:02.918
there's a common play space so in terms of
raising kids there is a lot of support.

00:07:02.918 --> 00:07:07.436
And they tend to pool resources in a truly
business school way to kind of get through

00:07:07.436 --> 00:07:10.199
the day and create more space and
more community.

00:07:10.199 --> 00:07:13.770
The other thing is SOs are very
integrated into the culture.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.360
I spend more time with some of my
classmate SOs than I do with them.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.646
There's a lot of events
after school hours,

00:07:19.646 --> 00:07:23.754
even during school hours where SOs are
welcome, both academic and more for fun.

00:07:23.754 --> 00:07:28.910
So they really are as much a part of
the experience as they want to be.

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:31.230
&gt;&gt; Great,
another question about admissions here.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.210
So how much weight is given
to one's academics and

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:37.230
work experience while applying for
an MBA from Stanford and

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.010
how much weight for
extra-curricular initiatives?

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:44.710
So for us,
we are not weighting anything or place

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:48.050
any stronger emphasis on any one part of
your application more than the other.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:51.050
We do read your application
completely holistically.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:54.410
We look at you and
treat you as a whole person.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.030
So there's not one thing in your
application that will be weighed

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:57.740
more than the other.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:00.360
We are really trying to
figure out all of the things

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.520
That you will be able to bring and
contribute to the class.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:07.480
Okay, so the next question,
back to our students, is can the students

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:11.230
talk a little bit more about classes
that they took outside of the GSB, and

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:13.464
maybe specifically at the d school,
the design school?

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.610
&gt;&gt; Sure, I'll start.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:17.120
&gt;&gt; Please, yes.
&gt;&gt; So

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.460
I am a joint degree student in the earth
and environmental sciences program.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.250
So I've taken a number of classes there,

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.990
one of which was a design school
class on the food system.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.100
I mean,
each class is going to be different.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.440
I loved both of the classes.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.580
I took a food economics class and a class
at the D School, and the main thing I will

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.980
say is it's a great chance to connect
with people outside the Business School.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:38.140
We call it the bubble.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.550
It is hard to get outside the bubble,
and classes is a great way to do that.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.607
The professors are obviously excellent,
and so

00:08:43.607 --> 00:08:46.733
it's really a matter of prioritizing
what is important for you to learn.

00:08:46.733 --> 00:08:49.984
A lot of classmates take computer
sciences classes, language classes.

00:08:49.984 --> 00:08:52.431
Maybe I'm able to speak more to that.

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:57.347
But the level's phenomenal and especially
in your 2nd year you almost have complete

00:08:57.347 --> 00:08:59.960
freedom to take whichever
classes you want.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:01.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, just to echo what you said.

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.320
I'm currently taking language classes.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:07.830
I took language classes this year which
was Portuguese, now I'm doing Chinese.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.260
That's something I like.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:13.050
The question's also specifically
about D school classes.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.610
I personally have not taken
a D school class yet.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.150
I do have it however as
a really big priority for

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.230
my next two quarters at the GSB.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.770
I felt the offering of the GSB was
satisfying a lot of my kind of

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:27.210
needs in terms of what
I wanted to experience.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.830
But I think for the next two quarters,
I really wanted to venture out.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:35.270
And on that note,
the D school has very often information

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:38.300
sessions where they present all of
the different classes that are available.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.260
And if it's not a class,

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:43.310
it's a pop-up class, which you do over the
weekend, dedicated to a specific topic.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:47.350
So there are different ways to kind
of engage with the D school program.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.480
&gt;&gt; Awesome.
Yes, well luckily our curriculum is

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:51.250
quite flexible.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:55.020
That allows our students to take a lot
of classes outside of our bubble.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:58.590
So the next question we have is how
did you decide to choose Stanford?

00:09:58.590 --> 00:09:59.540
want to start Omid?

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:04.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, how did, okay, let's maybe
just to preface a little bit, my answer.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.460
It's, Stanford was the only
school I applied to.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.000
And I actually applied two years in a row.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.520
I didn't get in the first time I applied.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.190
But I was so adamant and so

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:19.010
eager to get into Stanford that I did it
again and it worked out the second time.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:23.520
And the reason why I ended up applying
to Stanford was during my six years at

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:28.206
Google, a lot of the managers I had,
I had seven different managers,

00:10:28.206 --> 00:10:32.900
mangers or directors, and I think three or
four of them were actually GSB Alums.

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.040
I always knew about Stanford.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.680
I had a lot of admiration for Stanford but
because of those managers and

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.965
because of the leadership that
I experienced through them,

00:10:40.965 --> 00:10:46.230
this admiration for Stanford became
an aspiration, to become part of Stanford.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.720
And these leaders,

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:52.680
these managers were people that not
only influenced me professionally in my

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:57.240
thinking about my career, and how I show
up at work, but also very much personally.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.540
And that was, kind of seeing the way,
how they were impacting me and

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:04.400
shaping me, and hearing all these
anecdotes that they brought to work from

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.440
their time in business school,
that really got me pumped and

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.124
fired up to apply to business school so
much that I ended up applying twice.

00:11:13.124 --> 00:11:15.702
So, [LAUGH] involuntarily but still.

00:11:15.702 --> 00:11:16.705
&gt;&gt; It worked out.

00:11:16.705 --> 00:11:18.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
It worked out.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:21.610
&gt;&gt; So I'll talk very briefly about why
business school which is a big part

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:22.370
of why Stanford.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:26.040
I was working as I mentioned in
a non-profit in India on agriculture and

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:29.620
I was just very frustrated with
the current mechanisms for

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:31.070
making change in those ways.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:34.940
Policy as we've seen, is an intense and
often varying process and

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.460
so I wanted something that was much
more streamlined and more effective and

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.270
business school, going through
the corporate sector to influence

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:42.830
sustainability seemed
like the way to do that.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:47.200
With that in mind, I applied actually only
to environmental joint degree programs,

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:51.490
and so Stanford actually had a program I'd
looked at for a PhD many years before.

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:54.848
And now I'm doing a joint
degree with the program.

00:11:54.848 --> 00:11:59.540
So that was an easy fit, but honestly, one
of the most clear memories I have of when

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:02.390
I was like Stanford is
it was an info session.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:04.460
I came to campus, I'd been working in SF.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:08.180
I came to the campus for an info session,
which is part of why I love doing these.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:13.110
And was really interesting to talk to the
admissions facilitator, but then after,

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:16.000
actually one of the students who was
leading the session, came up to me.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.310
And was like I see you're from Israel, I'm
from Israel, do you want to grab coffee?

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:20.880
Can I answer your questions?

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.713
And she ended up connecting me
to people in the program and

00:12:23.713 --> 00:12:26.950
was just a real resource and
sense of support through the process.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:28.340
And she didn't know me at all.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:28.960
And that, for me,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:32.180
really reflected the sense of care and
commitment that people have here.

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:35.320
And honestly,
it's only gone up from there.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:37.280
&gt;&gt; Great.
And a quick followup question.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:38.030
As you mentioned,

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:41.010
doing a joint degree, how do students
typically balance doing that?

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:44.260
And do you have any kind
of advice on how to do so?

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:45.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So I, as I said,

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.970
I'm doing the Environment and
Resources joint degree.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.930
There's people doing law degrees,
medicine, computer science,

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:53.300
engineering, a whole range.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:56.110
And each of those experiences
are going to be different.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:59.210
In general, the program is actually
really well set up to accommodate.

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:02.970
There's almost no pressure or
no additional work that I have to do.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.110
It takes longer.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:07.490
In my case it takes extra,
from one extra quarter to an extra year.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:09.630
And it depends on how
you balance your units.

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.010
But it really comes down to choices.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.890
And so you make choices around how
you want to use your course time.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:16.919
And do you want to take
more business classes or,

00:13:16.919 --> 00:13:18.462
or do you want to go across the street?

00:13:18.462 --> 00:13:21.655
And those choices will depend how
much longer I end up spending here.

00:13:21.655 --> 00:13:23.965
But then a lot of other programs,
they just integrate it perfectly.

00:13:23.965 --> 00:13:27.225
And while you're here, you're here, and
then the rest of the time you're there.

00:13:27.225 --> 00:13:29.507
So it's been very smooth at least for me.

00:13:29.507 --> 00:13:32.887
&gt;&gt; Awesome, great,
another quick admissions question, so

00:13:32.887 --> 00:13:37.015
would you recommend getting
a recommendation [LAUGH] from a professor?

00:13:37.015 --> 00:13:39.950
What if you're applying
as a college senior?

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:40.690
So typically,

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:44.880
we tell people to shy away a bit from
getting recommendations from professors.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:49.100
Typically, they do not provide us with the
level of leadership potential that we're

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.828
looking for, the level of
detail that we're looking for.

00:13:51.828 --> 00:13:56.195
The biggest kind of exception to that is
if you are working with a professor doing

00:13:56.195 --> 00:13:58.945
research or anything like that, so

00:13:58.945 --> 00:14:03.725
if you are working with a professor
outside of class, maybe as a TA or

00:14:03.725 --> 00:14:08.115
teaching assistant or as a research
assistant then that may work for you.

00:14:08.115 --> 00:14:12.160
The biggest piece of advice I'd typically
give for choosing reccomnders, is to look

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:17.830
at the questions that we actually ask
them, that we will ask your recommender.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:22.260
And if that person, whether it's
a professor or someone else you know for

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:27.020
a fact can answer those questions with
a lot of detail, then that is okay for

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:28.820
you to use that person.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:31.969
So definitely use your judgment
when you're picking recommenders.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:34.959
Next question is about extracurriculars.

00:14:34.959 --> 00:14:38.449
What types of extracurriculars
are you involved in here on campus?

00:14:38.449 --> 00:14:40.517
&gt;&gt; I can take that.

00:14:40.517 --> 00:14:42.090
It's a good question.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:43.020
I do a lot, actually.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:47.030
So I'm on the board of four
different associations.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:52.950
One is the diversity committee, which is
dedicated to raising more awareness and

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:56.040
making the GSB a more diverse and
inclusive environment.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:59.580
Then there's the Asian Business Student
Association, I'm on the board of that.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:02.650
We actually have our identity retreat
coming up this weekend in Santa Cruz.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:06.630
There's 50 of us going there,
to discuss what it means to be Asian.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.550
For context,
we decided that Middle East, originally,

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:12.510
I'm from Iran,
is also geographically part of Asia.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:14.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just to explain why I ended up there.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.290
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Then there's the Student Alumni Board,

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:20.930
where we kind of develop
relationships with alumnis.

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:24.570
And I think the last one is
women in management which is

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.160
an organization very dear to me.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:33.380
We have a very large kind of movement
here on campus which is not only

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:37.340
by women to support and
empower each other, but also we

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:41.850
have an allied group which is women in
management, the main ally group of it.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:45.700
Where men come together and discuss how
we can be more supportive of women.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:50.255
And these four kind of activities plus
there are two trips I'm organizing,

00:15:50.255 --> 00:15:55.023
I mentioned, Italy one, I'm also taking
10 classmates to my home country,

00:15:55.023 --> 00:16:00.180
to Iran over Thanksgiving, and on a very
personal trip outside of school and

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:01.942
Things like that, so
there's a lot going on.

00:16:01.942 --> 00:16:05.681
There's really no limit to what
you can do extracurricular.

00:16:05.681 --> 00:16:07.282
&gt;&gt; Yeah, right, want to add anything?

00:16:07.282 --> 00:16:10.798
&gt;&gt; Yeah, as I was also involved with
WIM last year, and I'm the head of

00:16:10.798 --> 00:16:14.560
the Food and Ag Club, but I think
the most important extracurricular for

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:17.880
me right now is
the Arbuckle Fellows Program.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:20.203
It's a leadership program that
you do in your second year,

00:16:20.203 --> 00:16:23.322
and part of the program is to teach,
actually, a squad of first year students.

00:16:23.322 --> 00:16:26.161
Every first year student takes
leadership laboratories, and

00:16:26.161 --> 00:16:29.704
that lab is a group of six, which is your
squad, and it's led by a second year.

00:16:29.704 --> 00:16:33.134
You also do a series of coaching,
one-on-one with the students, and

00:16:33.134 --> 00:16:36.640
that's been just phenomenal opportunity
to develop skills as a coach.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:40.365
But also get to interact with first year
students and practice my leadership.

00:16:40.365 --> 00:16:43.930
I'll also just say on the side,
I am a yoga instructor, and so I was

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:48.605
teaching last year yoga classes every week
for classmates in our residency at Schwab.

00:16:48.605 --> 00:16:52.381
So there's just really unlimited ways
to give back and to get involved.

00:16:52.381 --> 00:16:54.099
&gt;&gt; And I still have to make it to a class.

00:16:54.099 --> 00:16:56.141
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They're over now.

00:16:56.141 --> 00:16:56.887
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:16:56.887 --> 00:16:58.129
&gt;&gt; Missed opportunity.

00:16:58.129 --> 00:17:00.617
&gt;&gt; Great, so for you specifically, Omieb,

00:17:00.617 --> 00:17:05.396
can you talk a little bit about what life
is like here as an international student?

00:17:05.396 --> 00:17:12.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I actually lived in the US
four years prior to starting at the GSB.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.190
And what it's like as
international student, I certainly

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:19.680
didn't have the experience of actually
having to move for the GSB to the GSB.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.410
Because I've also been living
in the Bay Area previously.

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:28.580
I really appreciate kind of the number
of international students we have,

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:31.020
personally I wish we had even more.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:34.954
I think I very much connect very
well with international students.

00:17:34.954 --> 00:17:39.565
We very much also celebrate the fact
that we come from different countries,

00:17:39.565 --> 00:17:41.085
we, is it this weekend?

00:17:41.085 --> 00:17:43.960
Yeah, on Sunday, we have a big food fair.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:48.030
There are 15 different countries preparing
food from their nationalities where we

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:51.660
kind of celebrate the diversity, the
geographic diversity we have at the GSB.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:58.100
And I think that's
a phenomenon that I've seen

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:02.492
at schools as well as in the workplace or
in my social circles outside of school.

00:18:02.492 --> 00:18:08.190
It's just people just tend
to bond possibly quicker and

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:11.830
in an easier fashion with
people of their own kind.

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:15.300
And I think these are tendencies,
as I said, you find everywhere.

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:18.800
But the good thing really that I
would like to highlight is that,

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.720
there's not too much of group formation.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:24.800
So people do come together to celebrate
a certain element of their identity.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.660
But at the same time, this element
is also celebrated by other groups.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:30.790
So we have a lot of cultural
events that are highly or

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:33.870
frequently visited by
also American students.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:39.054
So for example, the Asian Student
Business Association is very close with

00:18:39.054 --> 00:18:41.267
the African Business Student Association.

00:18:41.267 --> 00:18:44.894
So there's a lot of support between
the different identity groups.

00:18:44.894 --> 00:18:48.614
&gt;&gt; Yeah, anything to add at all,
just with your interactions at all?

00:18:48.614 --> 00:18:52.271
&gt;&gt; Yes, it doesn't really cross
my mind in a day to day way.

00:18:52.271 --> 00:18:55.170
Not because I'm not aware
of our diversity, but

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:59.061
because there's a really fluid
relationships between people of

00:18:59.061 --> 00:19:02.827
all places, American and
across all other kinds of identity.

00:19:02.827 --> 00:19:05.601
&gt;&gt; Great,
a question about faculty members.

00:19:05.601 --> 00:19:08.831
How accessible are facility members,
and do you have an example of

00:19:08.831 --> 00:19:12.996
a meaningful interaction you've had with
a faculty member since you've been here?

00:19:12.996 --> 00:19:14.436
You start.

00:19:14.436 --> 00:19:17.526
&gt;&gt; Yeah [LAUGH] a lot, honestly.

00:19:17.526 --> 00:19:23.739
So very accessible, I have to just very,
to say very honestly this week,

00:19:23.739 --> 00:19:29.065
I had a 50 minute twalk,
which is talk and walk, as I call it.

00:19:29.065 --> 00:19:30.968
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Those acronyms.

00:19:30.968 --> 00:19:32.106
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:32.106 --> 00:19:32.944
&gt;&gt; With Baba.

00:19:32.944 --> 00:19:33.861
&gt;&gt; With Baba?

00:19:33.861 --> 00:19:34.859
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:19:34.859 --> 00:19:35.823
[LAUGH] Sorry.

00:19:35.823 --> 00:19:37.239
&gt;&gt; It was a different professor, actually.

00:19:37.239 --> 00:19:41.767
But we just walked and
talked for 50 minutes.

00:19:41.767 --> 00:19:43.360
He's one of my professors.

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:46.230
I just dropped him a line and
said I want to to pick his brain on

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:49.260
some of the entrepreneurial projects
that I'm currently pursuing.

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:54.750
Yesterday, I signed up to host
a faculty dinner where one professor

00:19:54.750 --> 00:20:00.350
would come to our house, and we would host
a dinner for him and seven other students.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:01.827
I would say they are very accessible.

00:20:01.827 --> 00:20:03.871
They are always willing to meet.

00:20:03.871 --> 00:20:06.681
They are always willing to advise.

00:20:06.681 --> 00:20:08.130
I have seen in the past,

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:12.276
there have also been cases of
professors helping you with funding.

00:20:12.276 --> 00:20:12.983
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:20:12.983 --> 00:20:15.225
&gt;&gt; So it's very, very, very close.

00:20:15.225 --> 00:20:20.065
Not only do they know you by name very
quickly, again, because of the class size,

00:20:20.065 --> 00:20:23.222
but they also really care,
and I made it a priority for

00:20:23.222 --> 00:20:26.609
myself this year to meet as
many professors as possible.

00:20:26.609 --> 00:20:30.975
And they are absolutely willing,
and it's great, I love that aspect.

00:20:30.975 --> 00:20:31.834
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:20:31.834 --> 00:20:33.088
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:20:33.088 --> 00:20:35.072
Additional thing,
I completely agree with that.

00:20:35.072 --> 00:20:37.682
I think they also go in a very
wide variety of ways to invest in

00:20:37.682 --> 00:20:38.425
the community.

00:20:38.425 --> 00:20:39.883
Over the past couple of months,

00:20:39.883 --> 00:20:43.448
a very established politics professor
we have here, Keith Hennessey has been

00:20:43.448 --> 00:20:46.323
doing lunch time BBLs to discuss
the progress of the election.

00:20:46.323 --> 00:20:47.263
&gt;&gt; What is BBL?

00:20:47.263 --> 00:20:48.168
&gt;&gt; Brown bag lunch.

00:20:48.168 --> 00:20:50.386
[LAUGH] We use acronyms all the time here.

00:20:50.386 --> 00:20:53.716
And so that's just his way to kind of
give back and answer people's questions,

00:20:53.716 --> 00:20:54.720
and get involved.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.310
Similarly, there's a really
popular class you'll hear

00:20:57.310 --> 00:21:00.480
called managing growing enterprises,
taught by Joel Peterson.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:04.030
And there's some students who don't get
into the class because it's a lottery

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:07.990
system, so he, this quarter, ended up
hosting a series of seminars at his house.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:10.952
And so people who didn't get into his
official class were able to go to

00:21:10.952 --> 00:21:13.984
his house and kind of hear a lot of
the learnings that he shared with us.

00:21:13.984 --> 00:21:17.768
And that, just I think across the board,
people are really committed and invested.

00:21:17.768 --> 00:21:18.907
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:18.907 --> 00:21:20.910
&gt;&gt; Great, so how have, if at all,

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:25.013
have your career aspirations
changed at all from your time here?

00:21:25.013 --> 00:21:28.648
[LAUGH] How do you think about that?

00:21:28.648 --> 00:21:30.040
want to start, Tal Lee?

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:31.852
&gt;&gt; We're smiling because we
were just talking about that.

00:21:31.852 --> 00:21:36.580
[LAUGH] So
I have actually had a pretty big shift.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:39.969
I came in thinking I wanted to
sustainability specifically around farm

00:21:39.969 --> 00:21:43.932
sourcing and sourcing product in a more
environmental and socially friendly way.

00:21:43.932 --> 00:21:44.931
I did that this summer.

00:21:44.931 --> 00:21:47.308
I was at Blue Apron and
their farm sourcing team, and

00:21:47.308 --> 00:21:50.766
I realized in a lot of ways that the thing
that was interesting to me about that

00:21:50.766 --> 00:21:52.839
space was the people I
had been working with.

00:21:52.839 --> 00:21:55.390
I had been working in India with
a lot of small holder farmers.

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:58.005
And it was those connections
that had really motivated me and

00:21:58.005 --> 00:21:58.985
less of the food part.

00:21:58.985 --> 00:22:03.322
So I came back, and I kind of potentially
have scrapped that entire hypothesis, and

00:22:03.322 --> 00:22:07.172
I'm looking at much more people-focused
roles and kind of leadership and

00:22:07.172 --> 00:22:07.922
management.

00:22:07.922 --> 00:22:13.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I came with a vague
idea around entrepreneurship.

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:16.463
And I was very intentionally vague about
it because I think these two years,

00:22:16.463 --> 00:22:19.563
there's a lot of different
opportunities crashing into your life.

00:22:19.563 --> 00:22:23.708
Quite literally, a single conversation can
change the direction of your thinking.

00:22:23.708 --> 00:22:27.500
Be it with a classmate,
a class or a professor.

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:29.630
And I've been actually very open to that.

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:32.360
I still want to pursue entrepreneurship.

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:37.148
I nevertheless decided this summer
to work in venture capital because I

00:22:37.148 --> 00:22:39.315
spent my career in technology.

00:22:39.315 --> 00:22:43.440
But I wanted to move from the operational
side to the financial side of the table.

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:44.690
Realized that I don't like it,

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:49.200
which now makes me want to look back
into the operational side of things.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:53.941
And what I'm currently working
on is one idea is within

00:22:53.941 --> 00:22:57.041
a class called the Startup Garage.

00:22:57.041 --> 00:23:02.734
Which is a very experimental class
based on design thinking very much and

00:23:02.734 --> 00:23:04.050
to other ideas.

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:08.402
So it's still entrepreneurship,
and I think the beauty in general,

00:23:08.402 --> 00:23:12.606
is that you're exposed to a lot of
different kind of career paths and

00:23:12.606 --> 00:23:15.063
other people's career aspirations.

00:23:15.063 --> 00:23:18.587
I think you always need be a little
true to yourself, and when you enter

00:23:18.587 --> 00:23:22.660
the program, just have a rough idea
of where kind of your north star is.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:28.730
Because with all these different
people who are equally ambitious and

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:34.450
determined, it can very easily happen
that you get a little influenced by that.

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:36.810
Or being inspired is great, but

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:40.050
maybe being influenced by other people's
decisions is certainly not the best.

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:41.618
So you want to have your north star, but

00:23:41.618 --> 00:23:44.275
be open to kind of the inspiration
coming from your classmates.

00:23:44.275 --> 00:23:47.421
And I think that's how I've been faring so
far throughout the two years.

00:23:47.421 --> 00:23:51.309
&gt;&gt; And just really quickly, what are
the resources that you've been able to,

00:23:51.309 --> 00:23:54.724
hopefully been helpful to you,
and thinking about how you pivot?

00:23:54.724 --> 00:23:58.976
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we have a crew management
center, and there are career advisors, and

00:23:58.976 --> 00:24:01.020
I'd meet with one pretty regularly.

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:02.940
And I was teasing here
with a group of students,

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:04.900
like yeah, I see Grace every week.

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:06.540
And she stopped them and
she's like no, no, no.

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:08.155
Talia actually comes in to every week.

00:24:08.155 --> 00:24:11.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that's a very helpful resource.

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:14.100
And then they have a lot
of visioning workshops and

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.730
obviously a lot of different people
you can talk to, to help explore and

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:21.080
negotiate, and Walk through that process.

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:21.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:22.380
Awesome.

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:22.880
Thank you.
Okay.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.030
You already touched on this a bit,
Talia, about electives and

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:26.160
getting the classes you want.

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:28.410
So what is that process like
in choosing electives and

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.330
do you typically get
the classes that you want?

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:33.240
&gt;&gt; Shall I?

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:34.500
Me?
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:35.210
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:37.700
Do you always get the classes you want?

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:40.750
Most of the time, yes, but not always.

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:45.930
And I think an important lesson that I
learned throughout my time here is really

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:47.000
knowing what you want.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:51.080
And then once you really know what
you want, you prioritize that.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:54.590
There's a Election system basically.

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:59.430
You make those classes your priority and
you get them accordingly.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:01.750
I would, how I'm choosing electives?

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:05.260
The beautiful thing of the GSB is we have

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:08.060
a huge range of different
types of electives.

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:13.040
Classes that are focused on environmental
topics, Classes that are very data driven,

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:17.720
Classes that are very interpersonal,
kind of touching interpersonal topics.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:22.330
I just made the decision that,
I want to focus on my strengths,

00:25:22.330 --> 00:25:25.170
not to compensate my
weaknesses here at the GSB.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:27.940
So I'm not taking many
financial modeling classes,

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:32.160
I'm focusing more on philosophical,
interpersonal type classes.

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:36.150
And you know, there's a big variety,
my favorite elective so

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:40.820
far Has been winning writing with
Glenn Kramin, who he's a former

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:44.900
New York Times I think former editor
in chief if I'm not mistaken.

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:49.840
And he had this two week class about
how to become a better writer.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:54.630
Be it giving feedback to your employees,
be it an op-ed that you write Or

00:25:54.630 --> 00:26:00.580
other types of content called emails so
we rehearsed that.

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:02.190
And again, there's a big variety and

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:06.090
you can choose them by
just reading the reviews,

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:10.040
there's descriptions online but there's
also student reviews about the classes.

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:13.210
What I did I asked a lot
of my MBA two friends.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:13.910
What did they take?

00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:14.860
What do they recommend?

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:17.370
And they all responded and
gave me their lists.

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:20.590
And I took that as a starting point, yeah.

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:23.650
&gt;&gt; I'll just add another, my personal
favorite elected has been in her personal

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:25.930
dynamics, which is commonly
known as touchy-feely.

00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:28.305
But I am taking winning writing
next quarter, so who knows.

00:26:28.305 --> 00:26:29.979
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Good, you'll love it, it's great.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:32.020
Great.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:32.650
Awesome.

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:35.950
A couple of other quick questions
about admissions and financial aid.

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:39.582
So talking a little bit about financial
aid and how that works, our financial aid

00:26:39.582 --> 00:26:43.200
application happens after you have
already been admitted into the program.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:48.070
So once that happens our process
is need based, fully need based.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:51.650
What that means is,
after you are admitted you will get

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:55.190
information about the financial aid
process from our financial aid office.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:58.730
You will submit documents,
talking to us, and

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:01.330
helping us understand a little bit more
about your assets and your income.

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:06.415
And the level of fellowships that you will
receive will be based on that information.

00:27:06.415 --> 00:27:08.890
So whether you are a domestic student or

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:13.010
you're an international student,
you are eligible for fellowships.

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:16.460
We will also talk to you
about loan options as well.

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:21.146
The other question from financial
aid is talking a little

00:27:21.146 --> 00:27:24.820
bit about the Stanford USA MBA fellowship.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:28.440
This is a brand new fellowship that
we have launched this year and

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:31.710
with this fellowship it
is specifically targeting

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:34.790
students that have very
strong demonstrated ties

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:38.620
to the 12 states in the United States
that make up the midwest region.

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:41.340
So we have a ton of information
about this on the website,

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:45.230
we also have a recording of a previous
webinar we did about the fellowship so

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:47.900
if you have any further questions
you can ask about that.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:51.190
The specific question here is what
are we looking for in a candidate?

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:55.430
So with this process, you will apply to
the fellowship within MBA application.

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:59.880
So we're looking for edit baseline
the exact same thing were looking for

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:01.280
in the general MBA application.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:05.180
So the intellectual vitality,
demonstrate a leadership potential and

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:07.390
the personal qualities and contributions.

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:09.070
In addition to that, we are looking for

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:13.630
you to help us understand what your ties
are to the region and also the impact that

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:18.648
you want to have upon your return
to one of those 12 states.

00:28:18.648 --> 00:28:23.720
If you are interested in
the fellowship you must apply

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:26.390
by the round two deadline this year.

00:28:26.390 --> 00:28:30.050
And again for more information
definitely check out our website.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:34.500
Another question back to our students is
what has been your favorite experience at

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:35.970
Stanford so far?

00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:40.591
We'll start with that,
you want to start Talia?

00:28:40.591 --> 00:28:41.119
[LAUGH].

00:28:41.119 --> 00:28:41.682
&gt;&gt; Sure, goodness!

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:46.330
I guess the highlight, in essence what
I'm taking from that question, for

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:47.580
me I was getting a talk.

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:50.550
Talk is a tradition we have at
the GSB every Wednesday night.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:53.380
Two students come up and
give a talk on their life.

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:56.780
The origin, I just found out,
it was basically someone saying,

00:28:56.780 --> 00:28:58.710
" you know there's so
many incredible people here and

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:02.250
we don't really know that much about
them because everyone is so humble." And

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:06.070
Andy Dunn who was at JSA was like well
why then you don't talk about it?

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:08.250
And so it really is just a chance
to talk about who you are.

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:12.720
And I was fortunate to give I
think the third talk of the year

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:15.030
which was basically talking to
a bunch of people I didn't know yet.

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:17.670
And from that it was incredible
the number of emails and

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:20.260
kind words and people really
out to really get to know me.

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:23.370
And so it It made me feel very
safe in the community and

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:25.461
also very respected and valued.

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:31.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah, favorite experience, I gave
a talk as well and I agree with you Talia,

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:35.570
that is an extremely rewarding experience,
to share your story with your classmates.

00:29:35.570 --> 00:29:38.510
For me,
my highlight however was the GSB show.

00:29:38.510 --> 00:29:39.010
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:43.700
&gt;&gt; Which is a musical that
is entirely Written and

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:47.230
directed and produced by GSB students.

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:52.500
And it's a comedy you can say and
kind of making fun of

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:57.890
ourselves here at the GSB and that for me
was a highlight because not only because

00:29:57.890 --> 00:30:01.420
of the effort that went into it but also
because of this Really cool opportunity

00:30:01.420 --> 00:30:05.660
to be on stage with your classmates and
entertain thousand two hundreds,

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:10.890
professors, fellow classmates, significant
others, and so on and so forth.

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:13.036
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And it was very entertaining.

00:30:13.036 --> 00:30:13.930
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:15.230
What was your role?

00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:16.930
Did you sing, act, dance?

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:18.020
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:30:18.020 --> 00:30:20.380
the funny thing is I was
actually only acting.

00:30:20.380 --> 00:30:22.868
But They gave us microphones.

00:30:22.868 --> 00:30:28.800
So I had a microphone on my face
all the time and I was dancing.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:32.500
And while we were dancing,
some people in the front were singing so

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:34.830
everybody thought I was also singing.

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:38.890
So everybody was kind of congratulating
me on how great of a singer I am but

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:43.024
they didn't know that microphone was
actually off while I was dancing.

00:30:43.024 --> 00:30:44.665
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Personal things, so.

00:30:44.665 --> 00:30:46.350
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Awesome, great.

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:47.250
Okay.

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:51.570
So question about what is the best way to
prepare to get the most out of Stanford

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:58.180
for those who are able to
be admitted to the program?

00:30:58.180 --> 00:30:59.390
Talia, You want to start?

00:30:59.390 --> 00:31:01.050
&gt;&gt; Sure.
I ask myself this a lot.

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:02.650
Still asking myself this.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:05.430
Time is quickly ticking away
on this incredible experience.

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.640
I think feedback I got that was
super helpful was to spend time

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:10.130
with your loved ones.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:13.690
I cannot underestimate how intense of
an experience this is and how little time

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:16.910
you will have to spend with friends and
family outside of this world.

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:20.200
Not because you don't want to, but
because your day is just incredibly full.

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:21.090
So really,

00:31:21.090 --> 00:31:24.120
if you can, take the summer or the year
before to spend time with people you love.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:25.870
And the other thing is to prioritize.

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:28.350
Things will change as we've discussed, but

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:31.870
if you have a clear sense of what you
are here to get, it'll be that much easier

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:35.160
to make the decisions that flow
in almost on an hourly basis.

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:38.720
There are so many opportunities with every
hour of your day here and without a clear

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:42.460
sense of what your priorities are, it's
really easy to get swept away into things

00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:45.540
that are really cool and really valuable,
but maybe not what you're here to get.

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:48.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I really want to echo that.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:51.730
And I kind of mentioned
earlier as the North Star.

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:56.590
I feel if you come to school,
you should have a general idea.

00:31:56.590 --> 00:32:00.590
You should have a really good idea of
who you are, what your values are, and

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:03.480
kind of stay true to them
throughout the experience.

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:06.800
As I said, I think there's a lot
of There's social pressures.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:07.970
There's social influences.

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:11.110
A lot of things you're exposed to
that kind of shape your thinking.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:14.545
They can really drag you into
all different directions I feel.

00:32:14.545 --> 00:32:20.060
But if you know what you came here for
and what you want to get out of it,

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:25.640
As Tal Lee said, if you know your
priorities, in advance that really helps

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:30.950
make good decisions and
be happy with the decisions we make.

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:35.970
&gt;&gt; Great, okay well we
are coming up on 45 minutes, but

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:40.360
before we go just a couple last things
almost tied to the previous question.

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:41.870
Any final words of advice for

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:45.640
people who are still trying to figure
out whether an MBA is right for them.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:49.100
Do you want to start for me please.

00:32:49.100 --> 00:32:50.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah it's a tricky question.

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:53.070
I, there is no right answer,
I don't think.

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:57.190
There is no right applicant or
right student for an MBA.

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:02.260
An MBA is such a diverse program and
such a diverse opportunity that

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:05.970
people from all different walks of
life can get something out of it.

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:12.940
I once, someone once told me you
get an MBA if you want to lead,

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:16.470
if you want to be the executive
of a large organization.

00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:19.190
And I had that in the back of
my mind when I came to the GSB.

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:22.300
But I think at the GSB,
I realized that it's not true.

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:26.560
For me personally, an MBA is
an opportunity to challenge my thinking,

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:31.610
to make amazing new friends,
to take classes that push me.

00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:36.960
And really also just have some
intellectual kind of freedom to

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:42.050
pursue things that I at my time at
Google would have never considered.

00:33:42.050 --> 00:33:46.080
Just one quick example, I was working
on developing an investment thesis for

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:48.950
Iran, and I wanted to pursue
that over the summer and

00:33:48.950 --> 00:33:50.800
I worked on it with a professor.

00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:54.540
But that's something I would have never
even considered while I was at Google.

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:58.480
But at the GSB, you're exposed to so many
great things that it makes it possible.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:02.780
Point being, I think the MBA
gives you a lot of opportunities.

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:05.970
It really comes down to you If you
want to take advantage of those or not.

00:34:07.010 --> 00:34:07.670
&gt;&gt; I think that's great.

00:34:07.670 --> 00:34:10.660
The only thing I will add is in
terms of whether or not it's right,

00:34:10.660 --> 00:34:14.580
or when it's right,
really just try to figure out who you are.

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:17.230
And I say that because that's a huge
part of the application process.

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:18.900
It's also a huge part of what we do here.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:22.470
I would say more than half of my time
is spent on reflection and feedback,

00:34:22.470 --> 00:34:24.950
to better understand what my strengths
are and what my values are, and

00:34:24.950 --> 00:34:26.830
then to improve upon those.

00:34:26.830 --> 00:34:30.010
That comes into play very strongly
in the application as well.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:32.550
So as much time as you have,
given to making that decision.

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.760
I would say personally
I allocated a lot for

00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:37.690
that time towards just figuring
out who I was and what I wanted.

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:42.030
And from that I think naturally
decision hopefully will flow.

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:45.800
And in terms of timing, I started my
application in October for round two.

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:47.890
I thought I should just wait a year and
then forget it.

00:34:47.890 --> 00:34:51.610
And then I said who cares I'm just
going to give it a try and it worked out.

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:55.270
So not saying that's the right path but
certainly don't limit yourself just

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:57.580
because you don't think it's
the right from external pressures.

00:34:59.910 --> 00:35:03.330
&gt;&gt; Great, well thank you so
much Tal Lee and Omid for

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:06.440
joining us and
all of you around the world for watching.

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:09.680
You can learn more about the MBA
program on our website and

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:10.790
by watching our videos on YouTube.

